4. PLATFORM-CAPABILITY / PRE-EXISTING-BLOCKER EVIDENCE (why `test` is BLOCKED)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Platform IS fully capable: SwiftUI + Swift Charts + MapKit, iOS 18 / macOS 15
toolchain present; the WHOLE APP builds on BOTH platforms (section 2). So this is
NOT a capability gap and NOT a fault of this surface.

The blocker is that the base repo's shared XCTest bundle does not compile under
Swift 6 — a PRE-EXISTING condition in files this prompt is NOT allowed to touch
(allowed files = QuietHoursPanel.* only). Confirmed by the compiler across three
real test-bundle builds; representative errors:

  a) Non-Sendable global/static localize helpers (Swift 6 concurrency):
     - PresetGallery.ModelTests.swift / PresetGallery.Tests.swift
         "let 'passthroughLocalize' is not concurrency-safe because non-'Sendable'
          type '(String, String) -> String' may have shared mutable state"
     - SlideRenderer.FixturesTests.swift  ("static property 'echo' ... not
          concurrency-safe ...")
     - ActiveSessionsSection.{Tests,ModelTests}.swift, AnomalyInlineRow.{Tests,
       ModelTests}.swift, NoVehicleSelected.{Tests,ModelTests}.swift,
       ScheduledExportsPanel.Tests.swift  (same 'passthroughLocalize' pattern)
  b) Duplicate XCTestCase class names across sibling surfaces (redeclaration):
     - ChargeStatusWidget.Tests.swift  vs  ChargeStatusLiveWidget.Tests.swift
         ("invalid redeclaration of 'ChargeStatusAdapterTests'" + 4 peers)
     - SafetyFeaturesWidget.ModelTests.swift vs SafetyHistoryWidget.ModelTests.swift
     - DrivingCoachSection.ModelTests.swift vs DrivingCoachWidget.Tests.swift
     - MonthlyCostTable.Tests.swift vs MonthlyCostChart.Tests.swift

This surface's two test files use ONLY instance-scoped helpers (e.g.
`private let fallback` inside an @MainActor XCTestCase) and uniquely-named test
classes, so they are Swift-6-clean (proven by SWIFTC_TESTS_EXIT=0 above and by
contributing zero errors to every test-bundle compile). Fixing the ~dozen base
files would require editing non-owned surfaces — outside the allowed-files scope.

